// tests/cpu-limit.py (`%{cpu_limit}`), the CPU-time ceiling of the size runs
// of the long suite, in units of the `version` run of the same program, on
// both sides. The program here (Inputs/burn.py) burns its argument in
// seconds of CPU; its `version` only starts. A run that burns 0.05 s passes a
// ceiling of 15 (about 3.5 alone), the same run ten times slower fails it; the
// exit status must be the expected one.
// RUN: printf '#!/bin/sh\nexec %python %S/Inputs/burn.py "$@"\n' > %t.burn
// RUN: chmod +x %t.burn
// RUN: %{cpu_limit} 15 15 -- %t.burn 0.05
// RUN: not %{cpu_limit} 15 15 -- %t.burn 0.5 2> %t.err
// RUN: FileCheck %s --check-prefix=SLOW < %t.err
// SLOW: cpu-limit: {{[0-9.]+}} times the CPU time of `gopp version`, over the ceiling of 15.0
// RUN: %{cpu_limit} 15 15 --status 3 -- %t.burn 0 3
// RUN: not %{cpu_limit} 15 15 -- %t.burn 0 3 2>&1 | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=STATUS
// STATUS: cpu-limit: exit status 3, expected 0
